<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> pack kids in three layers (a moisture-wicking base layer, a fleece mid layer, and a waterproof jacket and pants), plus wool or synthetic ski socks, waterproof mittens, a neck gaiter, goggles, sunscreen and snacks. Rent skis, boots and often helmets at the resort, since kids outgrow them every season, but bring goggles and mittens that fit. Never put kids in cotton next to the skin, and pack a spare set of gloves and socks for every child.</p></div>

<%= render "shared/packing_checklist", kids: true %>
<%= render "shared/checklist_cta" %>

<h2>The layering system for kids</h2>
<p>Kids get cold faster than adults and won't always tell you until they're miserable. Layers let you adjust through the day.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Base layer:</strong> long underwear top and bottom in merino wool or synthetic fabric. It moves sweat away from the skin. No cotton, which stays wet and cold.</li>
  <li><strong>Mid layer:</strong> a fleece or light insulated jacket. Add a second on very cold days, or skip it in spring.</li>
  <li><strong>Outer layer:</strong> waterproof, insulated ski jacket and ski pants or bibs. Bibs keep snow out of little kids' backs when they fall or sit in the snow.</li>
  <li><strong>Socks:</strong> one pair of thin to midweight ski socks. Two pairs or thick cotton socks make boots tight, which cuts circulation and makes feet colder.</li>
</ul>
<p>More detail in <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %> and <%= link_to "the best base layer for skiing", guide_path("best-base-layer-for-skiing") %>.</p>

<h2>Packing by age</h2>
<h3>Toddlers and preschoolers (about 2 to 5)</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>One-piece snowsuit or bibs and jacket.</strong> One-pieces are warm but harder for bathroom breaks; bibs plus a jacket are a good compromise.</li>
  <li><strong>Mittens, not gloves,</strong> with long cuffs or wrist leashes so they stay on. Pack two pairs.</li>
  <li><strong>Neck gaiter or balaclava</strong> that fits under the helmet.</li>
  <li><strong>Extra full change of clothes</strong> in the ski school bag, including underwear.</li>
  <li><strong>Hand warmers</strong> for you, not for small hands inside mittens unless the product says it's safe for kids.</li>
  <li><strong>Diapers, wipes and any comfort item</strong> ski school asks for. Check the program's list before you go.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Kids (about 6 to 12)</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Full layering system</strong> above, with two base layer sets for a multi-day trip.</li>
  <li><strong>Waterproof mittens or gloves.</strong> Mittens stay warmer; older kids may want gloves for zippers and poles. See <%= link_to "ski gloves vs mittens", guide_path("ski-gloves-vs-mittens") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Small snacks in a zip pocket:</strong> energy dips fast, especially in the cold.</li>
  <li><strong>Name labels</strong> on everything. Ski school cubbies fill up with identical black mittens.</li>
  <li><strong>A card with your phone number</strong> in a jacket pocket.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Teens</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Adult-style layers,</strong> including a warmer mid layer for chairlift rides.</li>
  <li><strong>Gloves with liners,</strong> goggles with a second lens for flat light.</li>
  <li><strong>Phone, charger and a small battery pack.</strong> Cold drains phone batteries fast; keep it in an inside pocket.</li>
  <li><strong>Sunscreen and SPF lip balm</strong> they'll actually use.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Rent or pack?</h2>
<table>
  <caption>Kids' ski gear: what to rent at the resort and what to pack</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Item</th><th scope="col">Usually</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Skis or snowboard</th><td>Rent</td><td>Kids outgrow them fast, and flying with them adds bag fees.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Ski boots</th><td>Rent (or seasonal lease)</td><td>Feet grow every year. A good shop fit matters more than owning.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Helmet</th><td>Rent or pack</td><td>Rentals are fine; pack your own if it fits well and you're driving.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Goggles</th><td>Pack</td><td>Need to fit the child's face and helmet; resort prices are high.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Mittens and gloves</th><td>Pack (two pairs)</td><td>Wet mittens end ski days. Not usually rentable.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Jacket and pants</th><td>Pack</td><td>Some shops rent outerwear, but hand-me-downs and ski swaps are cheaper.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Base layers and socks</th><td>Pack</td><td>Personal items, never rented.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Check sizes before you book rentals with the <%= link_to "ski size chart", ski_size_chart_path %>, <%= link_to "ski boot size chart", ski_boot_size_chart_path %> or <%= link_to "snowboard size chart", snowboard_size_chart_path %>, and read <%= link_to "kids' ski gear", guide_path("kids-ski-gear") %> for what's worth buying.</p>

<h2>Helmet and goggle fit tips</h2>
<h3>Helmets</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Snug, not tight.</strong> The helmet shouldn't move when your child shakes their head, and it shouldn't leave red marks.</li>
  <li><strong>Sits level,</strong> about a finger's width above the eyebrows, not tipped back on the forehead.</li>
  <li><strong>Chin strap</strong> snug enough that only a finger or two fits underneath.</li>
  <li><strong>Try it with the gaiter or balaclava</strong> they'll wear underneath.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Goggles</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No gap</strong> between the top of the goggles and the helmet brim. A gap means cold forehead and fogging.</li>
  <li><strong>Seal all around the face</strong> without pressing on the nose.</li>
  <li><strong>Strap fits over the helmet,</strong> not under it.</li>
  <li><strong>Pick a lens for the conditions:</strong> a darker lens for sunny days, a lighter lens for snow and flat light. See <%= link_to "best ski goggles", guide_path("best-ski-goggles") %> and <%= link_to "best ski helmet", guide_path("best-ski-helmet") %>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Flying with kids: carry-on essentials</h2>
<p>Bags get delayed. Put what you can't easily replace, and what you need on day one, in your carry-on:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Ski boots</strong> if you own them, plus goggles and helmets if there's room</li>
  <li><strong>One full ski outfit per child:</strong> base layer, socks, mittens and gaiter, so they can ski even if checked bags are late</li>
  <li><strong>Medications</strong> and any kids' pain or fever reliever you use</li>
  <li><strong>Snacks and an empty water bottle</strong> to fill after security</li>
  <li><strong>Formula, breast milk and baby food:</strong> <a href="https://www.tsa.gov/travel/special-procedures/traveling-children" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">TSA allows these in carry-ons in quantities over the usual liquids limit</a>. Tell the officer at the start of screening.</li>
  <li><strong>Chargers, headphones and something to do</strong> for the flight and the drive to the mountain</li>
</ul>
<p>Weighing whether to fly at all? See <%= link_to "drive or fly to a ski resort", guide_path("ski-trip-drive-or-fly") %>.</p>

